# WebMonitor help

WebMonitor is a self-hosted dashboard for a home or small-office network:
uptime, what every device is, switches and Wi-Fi, traffic, alerts, and OS
updates through a small agent. Everything runs on your own server.

## Install

- [System requirements](requirements.md): hardware, OS, ports and what devices need
- [Install on Rocky Linux](install-rocky.md): the install script, upgrades, backups
- [Install with Docker](install-docker.md): Docker Compose, settings, upgrades, backups
- [Local test copy](local-testing.md): try it, test an upgrade or work on the code on your PC

## Use

- [Getting started](getting-started.md): first steps after signing in
- [Switches and SNMP](switches-and-snmp.md): port maps, server details, configuration backups
- [NetFlow](netflow.md): traffic reports and per-port flows, with switch configuration examples
- [Network tab](network-tab.md): live switch map and internet speed tests
- [Alerts and services](alerts-and-services.md): notifications and service checks
- [Agents and updates](agents-and-updates.md): the agent, installing updates, update windows
- [Devices and security](devices-and-security.md): device inventory, scans, Home Assistant, vulnerability scans
- [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md)
WEBMON_FILE_EOF

mkdir -p "$(dirname "$APP_DIR/docs/agents-and-updates.md")"
cat > "$APP_DIR/docs/agents-and-updates.md" <<'WEBMON_FILE_EOF'
# Agents and updates

The agent is a small program for Linux and Windows servers and PCs. It reports
CPU, memory, disks, inventory, failed services, workloads (Proxmox guests,
Docker/Podman containers) and pending updates, and it installs updates or
reboots when you ask or on a schedule.

It only connects out to WebMonitor over HTTPS; nothing listens on the host. It
only runs a fixed set of jobs, so the server can't make it run arbitrary commands.

## Install an agent

1. Open the host's card → **Agent** → **Install agent** (admins).
2. Pick Linux or Windows. WebMonitor shows a one-time install command with an
   enrollment token and the server's certificate fingerprint.
3. Run it on the host: as root on Linux, or in an elevated PowerShell on Windows.

The card shows the agent's data within a minute. The command works once and
expires after 24 hours; make a new one for each host.

- **Linux:** installs `webmonitor-agent.py` as a systemd service. Needs Python
  3.7+. Updates work on apt and dnf/yum systems.
- **Windows:** installs `webmonitor-agent.ps1` as a scheduled task. Needs
  Windows PowerShell 5.1+.

The agent trusts the server by its certificate. If the server's certificate
changes (a reinstall without restoring it, or a move to Docker without copying
it), reinstall the agents.

## Install updates

The **Updates** tab lists every host with an agent and its pending updates.
Select hosts, then:

| Action | What it does |
|---|---|
| Check for updates | Refreshes the list now |
| Install security updates | Security updates only |
| Install all updates | Everything pending |
| Reboot | Restarts the host |
| Upgrade agent | Installs the server's current agent version |

Tick **Reboot afterwards if required** to restart only when the updates need it.
**Job history** shows each job's progress and output.

## Update windows

**Settings → Schedules** sets recurring update windows: which hosts, which day
and time, security or all updates, and whether to reboot. Times use the
server's timezone. WebMonitor warns about clashes, such as a Proxmox host
rebooting while its VMs are being updated, or the WebMonitor server rebooting
while other hosts' jobs run.

## Remove an agent

On Linux, run the installer with `--uninstall` (`sudo bash install.sh --uninstall`),
or `sudo systemctl disable --now webmonitor-agent` and delete
`/usr/local/lib/webmonitor-agent`. On Windows, delete the **WebMonitor Agent**
scheduled task and its folder. Then remove the agent from the host's card.
WEBMON_FILE_EOF

mkdir -p "$(dirname "$APP_DIR/docs/alerts-and-services.md")"
cat > "$APP_DIR/docs/alerts-and-services.md" <<'WEBMON_FILE_EOF'
# Alerts and services

## Where alerts go

**Settings → Notifications → Send alerts to → Add a destination**:

| Type | Settings |
|---|---|
| Email | SMTP server, port, encryption, login, from and to addresses |
| ntfy | Topic URL (on ntfy.sh or your own server), optional access token. Subscribe to the same topic in the ntfy phone app. |
| Webhook | A URL. Discord and Slack webhook URLs get messages in their own format; any other URL (Teams, Home Assistant, your own) gets JSON with `title`, `text` and `priority`. |

Each destination can be turned off and on with its checkbox, and has a
**Send test** button. Alerts found in the same minute are sent as one message, so an outage
that takes 20 hosts down is one alert, not 20.

## When to alert

**Settings → Notifications → When to alert**:

| Alert | Default |
|---|---|
| Host down | After 3 minutes |
| Agent offline | After 5 minutes |
| Disk space | Warning at 85%, critical at 95% |
| Failed services (agent) | On |
| Antivirus off or out of date (Windows agent) | On |
| Monitored service down, certificate expiring | On |
| Reboots | On (crashes always alert) |
| Switch configuration changed | On |
| High bandwidth (NetFlow) | 50 Mbit/s for 15 minutes, or 50 GB a day |
| Internet speed below a minimum | Set on the Network tab |
| Send "resolved" messages | On |
| Remind about open problems | Off |

**Recent alerts** and the **Delivery log** below show what was raised and sent.

To silence a device for a while (maintenance, say), open its card and use
**Mute**. Muted hosts don't alert.

## Services

The **Services** tab checks things on top of plain up/down. Add a check with
**Add check**, or from a host's card.

| Type | Checks |
|---|---|
| Web | A URL answers with the expected status, optionally contains a keyword, answers within a time limit, and its certificate isn't about to expire |
| Port | A TCP port accepts connections (`host:port`, or a port on the chosen host) |
| DNS | A name resolves, optionally to an expected answer |
| Agent | A systemd unit (Linux) or Windows service is running. These have a **Restart** button. |

Web, port and DNS checks run from the WebMonitor server with no agent. Problems
appear under **Problems** and raise a **Service down** or **Certificate** alert.
