cat > "$APP_DIR/docs/local-testing.md" <<'WEBMON_FILE_EOF'
# Local test copy

Run a private copy of WebMonitor on your own PC, to try it out, test a new
release before upgrading your server, or work on the code. It uses Docker (or
Podman) and doesn't touch a production install. See
[Install with Docker](install-docker.md) for the details behind each step.

## Start a test copy

You need Docker Desktop (Windows or macOS), or Docker or Podman on Linux.

```sh
git clone https://github.com/treelinesoftware/WebMonitor-Public.git webmonitor
cd webmonitor
cp .env.example .env
```

In `.env`, set at least:

```ini
WEBMON_DB_PASSWORD=any-long-random-value
WEBMON_ADMIN_PASSWORD=test-admin-password
```

Start it on Docker's own network, so it doesn't open ports on your whole machine
beyond the published ones:

```sh
docker compose -f compose.bridge.yaml up -d --build
```

Open `https://localhost:8443/webmonitor/` and sign in as `admin`.

The test copy really monitors whatever you point it at, so it can check your
network from your PC. Agents and switches should keep reporting to your real
server; only point a test switch or agent at the test copy.

## Test with a copy of your real data

To see your own hosts, history and settings in the test copy, load a database
backup from your server.

1. On the server, make a dump:
   - Rocky Linux install: `sudo mysqldump --single-transaction webmonitor | gzip > webmonitor-db.sql.gz`
   - Docker install: see [Back up and restore](install-docker.md#back-up-and-restore).
2. Put the server's database password in the test copy's `.env` as
   `WEBMON_DB_PASSWORD` (it's `DB_PASS` in the server's `config.php`). Saved
   switch logins only decrypt with it.
3. Load it into a fresh test copy:

```sh
docker compose -f compose.bridge.yaml up -d db
gunzip -c webmonitor-db.sql.gz | docker compose -f compose.bridge.yaml exec -T db sh -c 'mariadb -uwebmonitor -p"$MARIADB_PASSWORD" webmonitor'
docker compose -f compose.bridge.yaml up -d --build
```

> A copy of production data also sends alerts, runs scheduled update windows
> through enrolled agents and backs up switch configs. Before starting the app,
> consider turning off notifications and schedules in the test copy's
> **Settings**, or start it with your PC disconnected from the network those
> devices are on.

## Test an upgrade

With the test copy running your current version and a copy of your data:

```sh
git pull                 # or check out the release you want to test
docker compose -f compose.bridge.yaml up -d --build
docker compose -f compose.bridge.yaml logs app
```

The log shows the migrations that ran. Check the dashboard, then upgrade the
server.

## Work on the code

`compose.dev.yaml` serves the app straight from your checkout, so changes show
up on the next page load without rebuilding:

```sh
docker compose -f compose.bridge.yaml -f compose.dev.yaml up -d --build
```

- PHP, JavaScript and CSS changes apply on reload (background jobs pick them up on their next run).
- Changes to `docker/` files, or new migrations, need `docker compose ... up -d --build` or a restart.
- Run the tests that need no server: see [the README](../README.md#development).

## Clean up

```sh
docker compose -f compose.bridge.yaml down        # stop, keep the data
docker compose -f compose.bridge.yaml down -v     # stop and delete the test data
```
WEBMON_FILE_EOF

mkdir -p "$(dirname "$APP_DIR/docs/netflow.md")"
cat > "$APP_DIR/docs/netflow.md" <<'WEBMON_FILE_EOF'
# NetFlow

With NetFlow, WebMonitor shows who is using the bandwidth: top talkers,
per-device traffic, services and conversations (**Reports → Traffic**), and what
is using each switch port (**Network → a switch → a port**). It can also alert
on high bandwidth.

Switches and routers send flow records to WebMonitor on **UDP 2055**. NetFlow
v5, v9 and IPFIX are supported. sFlow is not.

## Where to export from

- **The core switch or router** sees everything that crosses it, including all
  internet traffic. Start there.
- **Access switches** also see traffic that stays on them (two devices on the
  same switch talking to each other), which the core never sees.

Exporting from several switches is fine. When a flow crosses two switches that
both export it, WebMonitor counts it once in the traffic totals, and still shows
it on each switch's ports.

For exact per-port data, include the **input interface** in the flow record.
Without it, WebMonitor matches flows to ports by IP address through the port map.

## Cisco Catalyst 9000 / 3850 / 3650 (IOS-XE)

```
flow record WM-RECORD
 match ipv4 protocol
 match ipv4 source address
 match ipv4 destination address
 match transport source-port
 match transport destination-port
 match interface input
 collect interface output
 collect counter bytes long
 collect counter packets long
!
flow exporter WM-EXPORTER
 destination <WebMonitor server IP>
 source <management interface, e.g. Vlan10>
 transport udp 2055
 template data timeout 60
!
flow monitor WM-MONITOR
 exporter WM-EXPORTER
 record WM-RECORD
 cache timeout active 60
!
interface range GigabitEthernet1/0/1 - 48
 ip flow monitor WM-MONITOR input
```

Apply the monitor to the access ports (or the VLAN interfaces). Leave out the
uplink to another switch that also exports, so its traffic isn't recorded twice.
If the switch rejects `collect interface output`, leave that line out.

## Cisco Catalyst 2960-X (IOS 15.2, LAN Base)

Same as above, but leave out `collect interface output`, and use the physical
ports (the 2960-X has no flow monitoring on VLAN interfaces). Check that your
image supports it with `flow record ?` in configuration mode. Some images use
`collect counter bytes` without `long`.

## Routers and firewalls

- **pfSense / OPNsense:** install softflowd (pfSense) or turn on NetFlow under
  Reporting (OPNsense), version 9, destination `<server>:2055`, on the LAN interfaces.
- **MikroTik:** `/ip traffic-flow set enabled=yes` and
  `/ip traffic-flow target add dst-address=<server> port=2055 version=9`.
- **Others:** send NetFlow v5/v9 or IPFIX to `<server>:2055`.

## Check it's working

**Reports → Traffic** shows the collector's state: when the last flow arrived and
from which addresses. Data appears within 2 minutes.

If nothing arrives:

- check the exporter's destination and that UDP 2055 is open on the server's
  firewall (the Rocky Linux installer opens it);
- with Docker, use host networking (`compose.yaml`) so flows arrive from the
  switch's own address;
- if `WEBMON_FLOW_EXPORTERS` (Docker) or `WM_FLOW_EXPORTERS` is set, the
  switch's address must be in it.

## Load

A switch exports one small batch of records per minute per active flow: usually
a few kbit/s. The server keeps per-minute summaries, not every flow.

## Bandwidth alerts

**Settings → Notifications → When to alert**: alert when a device averages more
than a set Mbit/s over a number of minutes, or uses more than a set number of GB
in a day. By default only internet traffic counts. To exempt a device (a backup
server, say), open its card → **Edit** → **Expected high traffic**.
WEBMON_FILE_EOF

mkdir -p "$(dirname "$APP_DIR/docs/network-tab.md")"
cat > "$APP_DIR/docs/network-tab.md" <<'WEBMON_FILE_EOF'
# Network tab

The Network tab has two parts: internet speed tests, and a live map of your
switches with the traffic on every link.

## Internet speed tests

Tiles show the latest download, upload, ping and jitter, with a chart of recent
tests underneath. **Run now** starts one within a minute (admins).

**Schedule…** on the speed test panel (admins):

| Setting | What it does |
|---|---|
| Schedule | Every 15, 30 minutes, 1, 2, 3 (default), 4, 6, 12 or 24 hours, or off |
| Engine | **Built-in** (speed.cloudflare.com, nothing to install) or **Ookla Speedtest CLI** if it's installed on the server |
| Alert when the last two tests are slower than | Download and upload minimums in Mbit/s |
| Keep results for | How many days of results to keep |

Each test downloads and uploads for about 8 seconds each way, so the data it
uses grows with your speed: about 100 MB at 100 Mbit/s, about 1 GB at 1 Gbit/s.
On a metered or capped connection, pick a longer interval.

To use Ookla's CLI on a Rocky Linux install, install the `speedtest` package
from Ookla's repository (see speedtest.net/apps/cli) so it's at
`/usr/bin/speedtest`, then pick it in the settings. The Docker image only has the
built-in test.

## The network map

The map shows the internet, your router, switches and access points, linked the
way they're cabled (from CDP/LLDP and MAC tables). Each link is sized and
coloured by the traffic on it right now, and animates in the direction the
traffic flows.

- Drag to pan, scroll or pinch to zoom.
- **Show devices** adds every device on each switch port.
- Click a switch for its panel.

Live traffic is read over SNMP every few seconds while the tab is open, and once
a minute in the background for history. It needs SNMP on each switch and router
(see [Switches and SNMP](switches-and-snmp.md)).

## A switch's panel

- **Faceplate:** every port, coloured by status and load.
- **Ports:** a live table of each port's traffic in and out, speed, VLAN and
  what's connected.
- Click a port for its **traffic chart**: live, or history over 24 hours to a year.
- **What's using it:** with [NetFlow](netflow.md), the devices, services and
  conversations on that port. The panel says whether the numbers are exact
  (the switch reports which port each flow came in on) or matched by IP address.
- **NetFlow from this switch:** what the switch is exporting, per interface,
  with a configuration snippet if it isn't set up for per-port data yet.

## Network load

The map and per-port tables read each switch's interface counters: a few small
SNMP requests every few seconds, only while someone has the tab open.
Background history is one read per switch per minute. Neither is noticeable on
a switch.
